1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A state is not a thing. It is a promise about what happens next.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Two states whose rows are identical: merge them and nothing changes.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Feed both states the same strings and compare the output sequences.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
So the question is: which states make the same promise? That is a partition.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Definition: p and q are equivalent when every input string produces the same output sequence from both.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
GATE CSE 1996 Q2.23: states B and C have different next states, yet they are equivalent.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
All 511 input strings up to length eight give identical outputs from B and from C.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
A and B are separated by the string 00. A single witness is all it takes to prove two states differ.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The 2025 paper machine: eight states, one input X, one output f.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Start from outputs: one input can only distinguish states whose output on that input differs.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Four output vectors, so four classes: {A,B,C,E} with 00, {D,H} with 10, {G} with 01, {F} with 11.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
This is P0. Its classes are the 1-equivalent states: those no single input can tell apart.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Take the class {A,B,C,E} and look where each member goes on each input.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
A and C go to F and B; B and E go to D and C. Different class vectors, so the class splits.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The rule: two states stay together only if, for every input, their next states lie in the same class.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Here the partition stabilises after one pass: five classes, so five states.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
A machine built to need more than one pass: two chains that converge on the same state.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
P0 has two classes; one pass gives three; the second pass gives four and stops.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Stopping early under-counts. Here it claims three states where the answer is four.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The number of passes equals the longest shortest distinguishing string, minus one.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
One state per class: {A,C}, {B,E}, {D,H}, {F}, {G}. The reduced table has five rows.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
The reduced table is well defined: every member of a class produces the same output and lands in one class.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
4,088 string evaluations against the original: no mismatch anywhere.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The minimal machine is unique up to renaming: the partition is the equivalence relation, and it is forced.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
n states need the ceiling of log base 2 of n flip-flops.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Eight states to five states: three flip-flops to three flip-flops. Nothing saved.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The table does shrink: sixteen transitions become ten, and every row is a row of logic.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The twin machine does save one: seven states to four, three flip-flops to two.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A Moore machine prints from the state. P0 is then built from each state's own output.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The same behaviour, detect 11: two states as a Mealy machine, three as a Moore machine.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Both of those machines are already minimal: partition refinement changes neither.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
One boundary: an incompletely specified machine is not decided by partition refinement.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Shape 1: "minimum number of states required" is a NAT, and the answer is the class count.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Shape 2: "which states are equivalent" is an MCQ, and the answer is a class, not a pair.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Shape 3: design from a description, then minimise. The design is not the answer.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
A check that costs one line: every state of the reduced machine must be reachable.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Four lines: output partition, then repeat the split rule until nothing changes, then count, then read the table.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Trap one: comparing rows instead of classes. Trap two: stopping after one pass.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Trap three: claiming a saved flip-flop. Trap four: mixing Moore and Mealy minima.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Trap five: don't-cares. Then write the answer, and check it is at most the rows you were given.

